'''Bethnal Green and Stepney''' is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|parliamentary constituency]] in [[Greater London]], which returned one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP)  to the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]] from [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983]] until it was abolished for the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997 general election]].

Further to the completion of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], the seat was re-established for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], largely based on the existing seat of [[Bethnal Green and Bow (UK Parliament constituency)|Bethnal Green and Bow]].

==History==
The constituency was only ever represented by the former cabinet minister [[Peter Shore]], noted for his opposition to the United Kingdom's entry into the [[European Economic Community]], who held the seat for the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] from its creation in 1983 to its abolition in 1997.<ref>[http://www.aim25.ac.uk/cgi-bin/vcdf/detail?coll_id=6019&inst_id=1&nv1=search&nv2= "Shore, Peter David, 1924-2001, Baron Shore of Stepney, politician"], Archives in London and the M25 area</ref>

== Boundaries ==

=== 1983–1997 ===
The constituency was made up of nine electoral wards of the [[London Borough of Tower Hamlets]]: Holy Trinity, Redcoat, St Dunstan's, St James', St Katharine's, St Mary's, St Peter's, Spitalfields, and Weavers. It was abolished in 1997, and largely replaced by the larger [[Bethnal Green and Bow (UK Parliament constituency)|Bethnal Green and Bow]] constituency, in line with the [[Boundary Commission for England|Boundary Commission's]] recommendation that one seat should be lost in the paired boroughs of Tower Hamlets and [[London Borough of Newham|Newham]]. 297 electors moved to the new [[Cities of London and Westminster (UK Parliament constituency)|Cities of London and Westminster]] constituency.

=== Current ===
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Bethnal Green and Stepney (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-width=200|text=Map of boundaries from 2024}}
Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the composition of the constituency is as follows (as they existed on 1 December 2020):

* The London Borough of Tower Hamlets wards of: Bethnal Green; St. Dunstan’s; St. Peter’s; Shadwell; Spitalfields & Banglatown; Stepney Green; Weavers; Whitechapel.

''Apart from [[Shadwell]] and a small part of [[Whitechapel]] ward, previously part of [[Poplar and Limehouse (UK Parliament constituency)|Poplar and Limehouse]], the constituency replaced [[Bethnal Green and Bow (UK Parliament constituency)|Bethnal Green and Bow]] - excluding [[Bow, London|Bow]], which was included in the newly created seat of [[Stratford and Bow (UK Parliament constituency)|Stratford and Bow]].''

