'''Croydon South''' is a [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[borough constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} created in 1974 and represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since 2015 by [[Chris Philp]], a [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, Croydon South elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}

==Political history==
In 1974, the [[Croydon South (historic UK Parliament constituency)|original Croydon South]] constituency created in 1918 was renamed [[Croydon Central (UK Parliament constituency)|Croydon Central]] and the current Croydon South was created from the northwestern part of [[East Surrey (UK Parliament constituency)|East Surrey]], covering [[Purley, London|Purley]] and [[Coulsdon]], which had become parts of [[Greater London]] in 1965.

Since its creation, the constituency has been represented by three Conservative MPs. [[William Clark, Baron Clark of Kempston|Sir William Clark]], who had represented East Surrey since [[1970 United Kingdom general election|1970]], won the new seat in [[February 1974 United Kingdom general election|February 1974]], and held it until his retirement in [[1992 United Kingdom general election|1992]]. His successor, Sir [[Richard Ottaway]], then held the seat until [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015]], when he stood down and was succeeded by [[Chris Philp]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Conservative MP Richard Ottaway to step down at election |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-20108756 |publisher=BBC News |date=27 October 2012 |access-date=9 February 2013 |archive-date=4 December 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121204233244/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-20108756 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=Decl2015>{{cite web|url=https://www.croydon.gov.uk/sites/default/files/articles/downloads/Croydon-South-election-results_0.pdf|title=DECLARATION OF RESULT OF POLL|date=7 May 2015|work=2011 Electorate Figures|publisher=London Borough of Croydon|access-date=8 May 2015|archive-date=3 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303235500/https://www.croydon.gov.uk/sites/default/files/articles/downloads/Croydon-South-election-results_0.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> Boundary changes have been minor and the 2015 result made the seat the 145th safest of the Conservative Party's 331 seats by percentage of majority.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ukpolitical.info/conservative-mps-elected-2015.htm |title=Conservative Members of Parliament 2015 |website=UK Political.info |access-date=2017-02-12 |archive-date=2017-06-08 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170608170823/http://www.ukpolitical.info/conservative-mps-elected-2015.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> At the 2015 general election, it was one of the Conservatives' safest seats in Greater London.<ref>{{cite web |title=Election 2015: Croydon South |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/politics/constituencies/E14000656#general_election_data-constituency_profile_en |publisher=BBC News |date=8 May 2015 |access-date=4 April 2017 |archive-date=12 June 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170612001339/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/politics/constituencies/E14000656#general_election_data-constituency_profile_en |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Constituency profile==
Croydon South consists of affluent [[suburbia]], including a significant minority of large houses with gardens on the [[North Downs]] escarpment. It has many well-to-do commuters taking advantage of the fast trains to the City and [[Gatwick Airport]], plus a significant proportion of retired people.

The village-like [[Selsdon]] – one of the few places in the seat where transport links are poor – has a place on the political map having spawned the phrase '[[Selsdon Man]]',{{citation needed|date=June 2017}} its [[swing (politics)|swing]] and voters first seen as an ideal [[bellwether]] for the national swing by the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]]. The Purley Way, which runs mainly through Waddon, has become home to large retail estates for out-of-town shopping and leisure. Waddon is the sole Labour ward in the constituency – one of its councillors, [[Andrew Pelling]], the former Conservative MP for [[Croydon Central]], defected to the Labour Party.

At the southern end of the constituency, [[Coulsdon]] has much in common with the residual county of [[Surrey]] of which it was a more intrinsically associated part until 1965 as it was excluded from [[Croydon County Borough]] on the county borough's creation in 1889.

