== Political history ==
The [[Labour Party (United Kingdom)|Labour Party]] has [[safe seat|safe]] majorities of more than a 15% share of the vote since the seat was created in 1997. The runner-up party in four of the seven general elections to date has been the [[Conservative Party (United Kingdom)|Conservative Party]], the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] twice, and the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] once.

When the constituency was created for the 1997 election, it was estimated that had the seat existed in [[1992 United Kingdom general election|1992]], Labour would have won it with a majority of less than 2,000 votes over the second placed Conservatives, making it a marginal seat. The 1997 result therefore suggested that the Conservative vote had halved since the previous election. This performance was poorer than the average fall of the Conservative vote in London and led the ''[[Almanac of British Politics]]'' to note that there was "now no question of this constituency being marginal."<ref name="Almancac6thp277">{{cite book |last1=Waller |first1=Robert |last2=Criddle |first2=Byron |title=[[Almanac of British Politics|The Almanac of British Politics]] |date=1999 |publisher=Routledge |location=London |isbn=0-415-18541-6 |page=277 |edition=Sixth}}</ref>

The constituency takes in the eastern side of [[Lambeth London Borough Council|Lambeth Council]] which include the wards of Coldharbour, Herne Hill, Thurlow Park, Gipsy Hill and Knight's Hill. It also takes in the south western end of [[Southwark London Borough Council|Southwark Council]] which encompass Dulwich Village, Goose Green and Dulwich Hill wards. The [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] have two councillors in constituency and are the official opposition on Lambeth Council. The Labour Party have twenty-one councillors.

== Members of Parliament ==
The constituency was created in 1997 from parts of the former seats of [[Dulwich (UK Parliament constituency)|Dulwich]] and [[Norwood (UK Parliament constituency)|Norwood]]. It was represented from its creation until 2015 by the former [[Secretary of State for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport|Secretary of State for Culture]] (2001–2007), [[Tessa Jowell]].

