'''Ealing Central and Acton''' is a [[List of United Kingdom Parliament constituencies|constituency]] created in 2010,{{refn|A [[borough constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since 2015 by [[Rupa Huq]],{{refn|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}} who was elected as a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] MP, suspended from the party in September 2022 following alleged racist comments,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/sep/27/rupa-huq-has-labour-whip-suspended-after-kwarteng-comments|title=Rupa Huq has Labour whip suspended after Kwasi Kwarteng comments|website=The Guardian|last=Allegretti|first=Aubrey|date=27 September 2022 |access-date=4 October 2022}}</ref> and reinstated in March 2023.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-64835061 | title=Labour MP Rupa Huq regains whip after Kwasi Kwarteng racism row | work=BBC News | date=3 March 2023 }}</ref>

==Constituency profile==
The seat takes in an eastern third of the [[London Borough of Ealing]] – including the commercial centres of Acton and Ealing. There are suburban residential side streets, educational establishments, small industrial estates, sports areas, part of the [[Grand Union Canal]] and parks, centred around the [[Uxbridge Road]] (A4020). This is one of the more affluent seats in London.<ref>Electoral Calculus https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=Ealing+Central+and+Acton</ref>

==Political history==
The [[Fifth Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] created the seat by selecting wards for the year 2010 to equalise electorates.  Here, if votes were cast as in 2005, this seat would have produced a three-way [[marginal seat|marginal]] between the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] (32.8%), Labour (32.6%), and [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] (29.7%) [[political party|parties]].<ref name=UKPollingReport>{{cite web|url=http://ukpollingreport.co.uk/guide/seat-profiles/ealingcentralandacton|title=UKPollingReport Election Guide 2010 » Ealing Central and Acton|work=ukpollingreport.co.uk|access-date=2008-01-05|archive-date=2007-12-23|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071223032921/http://ukpollingreport.co.uk/guide/seat-profiles/ealingcentralandacton|url-status=live}}</ref> An analysis of intervening local results indicated that the seat would, if no voters were swung nor new voters introduced, present a tiny Labour majority.
;2010 campaign
In the 2010 general election, [[Angie Bray]], a Conservative, won the seat with a majority of 3,716, representing a [[swing (politics)|swing]] from Labour to the Conservatives of 5%.{{refn|Based upon the notional outcome of an election fought with electoral wards from the various previous seats fought in the previous election.|group= n}} 
;2015
According to the [[BBC]], heavy campaigning in the 2015 general election was expected by leading figures and regional activists of the two largest political parties;<ref name="Hollins">{{cite news | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-32174174?SThisFB&fb_ref=Default | title=Ealing Central and Acton is key election battleground | work=[[BBC News]] | date=7 April 2015 | access-date=8 April 2015 | author=Hollins, Victoria | archive-date=26 September 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150926002754/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-london-32174174?SThisFB&fb_ref=Default | url-status=live }}</ref> at the time it was 56th on the list of Labour target seats.<ref name="Labour list">{{cite news | url=http://labourlist.org/2013/01/labours-106-battleground-target-seats-for-2015/ | title=Labour's 106 battleground target seats for 2015 | work=[[Labour List]] | date=8 January 2013 | access-date=8 April 2015 | archive-date=7 April 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407124801/http://labourlist.org/2013/01/labours-106-battleground-target-seats-for-2015/ | url-status=live }}</ref> In a mixed election for two-way targets of the two largest parties, Labour's [[Rupa Huq]] won the constituency. The 2015 result gave the seat the 2nd most marginal majority of Labour's 232 seats by percentage of majority.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ukpolitical.info/labour-mps-elected-2015.htm |title=Labour Members of Parliament 2015 |website=UK Political.info |archive-date=2018-09-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929214847/http://www.ukpolitical.info/labour-mps-elected-2015.htm |url-status=live}}</ref>
;2017
In April 2017, the Green Party announced that it would not stand a candidate in this constituency for the 2017 general election and instead lend its support to the sitting MP, [[Rupa Huq]].<ref name="Dan Bloom">{{cite news | url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/green-party-pulls-out-crucial-10281739 | title=Green Party pulls out of crucial general election seat to help Labour beat the Tories | work=[[Daily Mirror]] | date=23 April 2017 | access-date=28 April 2017 | author=Bloom, Dan | archive-date=27 April 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170427103718/http://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/green-party-pulls-out-crucial-10281739 | url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Felicity Morse">{{cite news | url=https://inews.co.uk/essentials/news/politics/local-election-pact-ealing-green-party-stand-aside-help-labour-mp-rupa-huq-win/ | title=Local election pact: Ealing Green Party stand aside to help Labour MP Rupa Huq win | work=[[i (newspaper)|i]] | date=23 April 2017 | access-date=28 April 2017 | author=Morse, Felicity | archive-date=29 June 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170629192910/https://inews.co.uk/essentials/news/politics/local-election-pact-ealing-green-party-stand-aside-help-labour-mp-rupa-huq-win/ | url-status=live }}</ref>

==Boundaries==

=== 2010–2024 ===
The constituency consisted of the following electoral wards of the [[London Borough of Ealing]]:
*Acton Central, Ealing Broadway, Ealing Common, East Acton, Hanger Hill, South Acton, Southfield, and Walpole

The constituency was created with an electorate close to the electoral quota of 69,703 for 2006.<ref>[http://www.official-documents.gov.uk/document/cm70/7032/7032_i.pdf Fifth Periodical Report] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121029101813/http://www.official-documents.gov.uk/document/cm70/7032/7032_i.pdf |date=2012-10-29 }}, Boundary Commission for England, page 7 {{ISBN|0-10-170322-8}}. Also contains list of boundary changes in England.</ref>

=== Current ===
Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], which came into effect for the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], the constituency is composed of:

* The London Borough of Ealing wards of: Ealing Broadway; Ealing Common; East Acton; Hanger Hill; North Acton; South Acton; Southfield.

* The London Borough of Hammersmith and Fulham wards of: College Park & Old Oak; Wormholt.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 3 London region}}</ref>

''The two Hammersmith and Fulham wards were added from [[Hammersmith (UK Parliament constituency)|Hammersmith]] (abolished), offset by the transfer of the Ealing Borough ward of Walpole to [[Ealing Southall (UK Parliament constituency)|Ealing Southall]].'' 

